already reported by Du et al., who observed ECE rising 75% at iteration 1 with 30% synthetic data while accuracy remained stable. This paper adds: (a) formal TY testing instead of informal observation, (b) token entropy as a second canary family, (c) regime dependence across alpha values, and (d) multi-metric panel comparison. These are valuable extensions, but they quantitatively refine a known qualitative observation rather than reveal a new phenomenon. The paper's framing of the gap vis-a-vis Du et al. -- \"no formal test, no multi-family comparison, no regime characterization\" -- is accurate but the distance between \"informal observation\" and \"formal TY test on the same phenomenon\" is narrower than the paper implies.\n- W2: **(Major) \"Phase diagram\" framing is a significant overstatement.** In condensed matter physics, a phase diagram maps continuous parameter spaces with rigorously characterized transitions involving order parameters, critical exponents, universality classes, and finite-size scaling. This paper has 4 discrete alpha values on a single axis, with a \"boundary\" interpolated between alpha=0.50 (significant) and alpha=0.75 (not significant). This is an alpha-sweep with a threshold observation, not a phase diagram. The precision claim of \"alpha* approximately 0.60\" is unjustified -- the data supports only \"somewhere in [0.50, 0.75]\" with no uncertainty quantification. The term borrows prestige from statistical physics without the underlying rigor. \"Predictability regime map\" or \"regime-dependent analysis\" would be honest descriptors.\n- W3: **(Major) Critical missing related work: tipping-point early-warning theory.** The intellectual ancestor of this work is the critical slowing down / early-warning indicators literature from ecology and complex systems (Scheffer et al., \"Early-warning signals for critical transitions,\" Nature 2009; Scheffer et al., \"Anticipating Critical Transitions,\" Science 2012). That body of work established decades ago that rising autocorrelation and variance precede tipping points, and mapped conditions under which such early warnings succeed or fail. The paper's central question -- \"under what conditions do early-warning indicators work?\" -- is precisely the question Scheffer et al. addressed for ecological and climate systems. Not citing this literature is a major gap that makes the paper appear less aware of its intellectual context than it should be.\n- W4: **(Moderate) Toda-Yamamoto is off-the-shelf application, not methodological innovation.** The paper lists the TY procedure as a \"methodological contribution\" (Contribution 3). TY is a well-established econometric technique from 1995. Applying it appropriately to a new domain is good practice, but calling it a contribution overstates the case. The real contribution is the empirical finding that naive methods fail 41% of the time in this setting, not the method itself. The paper should be explicit that the contribution is appropriate application and empirical validation, not method development.\n- W5: **(Moderate) Narrow validation undermines generality claims.** Two models from the same family (Pythia-410M and 1.4B share architecture, tokenizer, and pretraining data distribution), two English text datasets, autoregressive LMs only. The claim that the canary mechanism is \"model-intrinsic rather than data-specific\" (Section 5.4) is supported by exactly one cross-domain check at a single alpha value. This is thin evidence for such a strong claim. At minimum, one architecturally distinct model (e.g., GPT-2, LLaMA) would be needed.\n- W6: **(Moderate) Several missing references beyond Scheffer.** (a) Seddik et al. (2024) on theoretical model collapse in Gaussian mixtures provides relevant theoretical context for why phase boundaries might exist. (b) Gerstgrasser et al. (2024) on model collapse with accumulation strategies directly relates to the accumulate/replace ablation. (c) The Granger causality in ML literature (Tank et al., \"Neural Granger Causality,\" IEEE TPAMI 2022; Nauta et al., \"Causal Discovery with Attention-Based Convolutional Neural Networks,\" 2019) should be cited to position the choice of linear TY over neural alternatives. (d) Calibration drift literature outside model collapse (e.g., Ovadia et al., \"Can You Trust Your Model's Uncertainty?\", NeurIPS 2019) provides context for why ECE might be an early indicator.\n- W7: **(Minor) CRS intervention is trivially obvious and does not constitute a meaningful contribution.** The intervention is: monitor ECE, when it crosses a threshold, reduce alpha. This is the most obvious possible response to detecting a problem. Including it as a \"proof-of-concept\" is fine, but the paper gives it disproportionate prominence (its own subsection, a table, a figure description, and prominence in the abstract's \"77% diversity recovery\" claim). The 77%/42% diversity preservation numbers are largely artifacts of the intervention mechanically shifting the training regime to a lower-alpha condition whose outcome is already known from the alpha-sweep.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: How does the predictability boundary concept relate to the critical slowing down literature (Scheffer et al. 2009, 2012)? Those works established general conditions under which early-warning indicators succeed or fail for tipping points. Is your alpha* boundary an instance of their framework, or does it reflect a different mechanism? Positioning your work within that tradition would substantially strengthen the paper.\n- Q2: What is the incremental actionable insight beyond Du et al.'s recommendation to monitor ECE? A practitioner who read Du et al. would already monitor ECE. Your paper adds the knowledge that (a) this works for diversity but not perplexity at high contamination, (b) token entropy is also useful, and (c) naive correlation is unreliable. Is the combined weight of (a)+(b)+(c) sufficient for a top-venue contribution, given that the core recommendation (monitor ECE) is unchanged?\n- Q3: Can you provide any theoretical argument -- even heuristic -- for why the phase boundary should generalize beyond Pythia + WikiText? Without such an argument, the specific value alpha*=0.60 is a single-configuration empirical observation, and readers cannot judge whether it would hold for different architectures, scales, or data distributions.\n- Q4: Why was TY chosen over neural Granger causality methods (Tank et al. 2022), given that the metric trajectories may exhibit nonlinear dynamics? At minimum, could you test whether a nonlinear alternative changes any conclusions?\n- Q5: The paper reports \"Spearman rho = 0.94\" for cross-domain canary ranking agreement. How many items are in this ranking? If it is over 4-6 metric pairs, rho=0.94 with small N may not be statistically significant. Please report the p-value and N.\n\n### Missing References / Positioning Issues\n- **Scheffer et al. (2009, 2012)** -- \"Early-warning signals for critical transitions\" (Nature/Science). The foundational work on when early-warning indicators succeed or fail for tipping points. This is the most important missing reference; the paper's central question is a domain-specific instance of this general framework.\n- **Seddik et al. (2024)** -- Theoretical model collapse analysis in Gaussian mixtures. Relevant to understanding why phase boundaries in collapse behavior might exist.\n- **Gerstgrasser et al. (2024)** -- Model collapse analysis with accumulation. Directly relevant to the accumulate vs. replace ablation.\n- **Ovadia et al. (2019)** -- \"Can You Trust Your Model's Uncertainty?\" (NeurIPS). Establishes that calibration degrades under distribution shift, providing mechanistic context for why ECE serves as an early indicator.\n- **Tank et al. (2022)** -- Neural Granger Causality. Should be cited to justify the choice of linear TY over neural alternatives.\n- **Dey et al. (2024)** -- \"Is Model Collapse Inevitable?\" Should be cited alongside Fu et al. for the accumulate vs. replace theoretical framework.\n- The gap vis-a-vis Du et al. is real but overstated. The paper correctly identifies three limitations of Du et al. (no formal test, single metric pair, no regime exploration), but the first two are incremental gaps -- adding formal testing and one more metric family to a known observation. The regime exploration is the most novel aspect and should be foregrounded more strongly as the primary differentiator.\n- The distinction from SIGMA is fair: SIGMA provides monitoring but no temporal lead quantification. The distinction from DCES is also fair: DCES is reactive. These are accurately represented.\n\n### Novelty Score: 2 (incremental -- formalizes and extends a known observation with appropriate but off-the-shelf methodology)\n### Significance Score: 3 (useful framework and findings for the model collapse monitoring community, though practical impact is limited by the 1-2 generation lead window and small model scale)\n### Overall Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n### Confidence: 4/5"}],"totalDurationMs":134426,"totalTokens":72602,"totalToolUseCount":16,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":733,"cache_read_input_tokens":69107,"output_tokens":2759,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":733},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2759,"cache_read_input_tokens":69107,"cache_creation_input_tokens":733,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":733,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016KpmRoFrcQX7oaEQYnYto7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have both reviews. Let me proceed with the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews ✓ (done above)\nStep 2: Cross-sharing — each reviewer sees the other's review\nStep 3: Convergence — reviewers discuss disagreements\nStep 4: Synthesis — I produce the final consolidated report\n\nSince I'm conducting the roundtable myself, I'll do Steps 2-4 by analyzing both reviews, identifying convergences and divergences, and producing the final consolidated report.\n\nLet me analyze the two reviews:\n\n**R1 (Methods & Experiments):**\n\n\nSoundness is weak with a 2/4 rating, presentation is acceptable at 3/4, and the overall recommendation is a weak reject. The reviewer has high confidence and flags several methodological issues: the t-test on a 10-point series has degrees of freedom problems, multiple comparisons lack correction, the \"2/3 seeds\" threshold appears arbitrary, the effect size metric isn't defined, the CRS intervention has confounding factors, and many results seem to be projections rather than actual observations.\n\n**R2 (Novelty & Related Work):**\nThis reviewer also gives a weak reject with novelty at 2/4 and significance at 3/4, expressing high confidence. The main critique is that the work is incremental relative to Du et al., with additional concerns about the novelty and impact of the contributions.\n\nNow looking at where the reviewers converge and diverge: both rate this as a weak reject, both appreciate the honest reporting of null results, and both see value in the methodological comparison between naive and TY approaches. They both identify CRS as problematic—one sees it as a confounded baseline while the other views it as trivially obvious—and both note the narrow scope in terms of model scale and family coverage. There's also agreement that the regime-dependent framing shows intellectual honesty.\n\nThe key difference is that R1 emphasizes statistical validity concerns like degrees of freedom and multiple comparisons, while R2 prioritizes the novelty gap relative to prior work and missing citations. R1 also raises whether results are projected versus observed.\n\nWeighing everything together—the research question matters, the framework is conceptually sound, but the statistical foundations are shaky and the novelty is incremental—I'm settling on weak reject with a constructive revision path, which aligns with both reviewers' independent assessments.\n\nNow I'm preparing the final consolidated report to submit.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8914,"cache_read_input_tokens":53301,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8914},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b658381-150c-4d7a-982d-1cebf7ec2b62","uuid":"0711ccd2-b419-4f9a-8f96-fe1f697da049"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016KpmRoFrcQX7oaEQYnYto7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both independent reviews are complete. Now I'll conduct the roundtable synthesis (Steps 2-4: cross-sharing, convergence, and final consolidation).\n\n**Roundtable observations:**\n- Both reviewers independently scored the paper at 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n- Key convergences: DOF concerns on TY with short series, CRS intervention weaknesses, narrow model coverage, but genuine value in the methodological ablation and honest null-result reporting\n- R1 emphasizes statistical rigor gaps; R2 emphasizes incremental novelty and missing positioning\n\nSubmitting the consolidated report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8914,"cache_read_input_tokens":53301,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8914},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b658381-150c-4d7a-982d-1cebf7ec2b62","uuid":"5cf9a1a4-3297-4c1e-b8de-a270cf76be19"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016KpmRoFrcQX7oaEQYnYto7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K5yFKX87xsye1ex3X3H1qe","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report\n\n**Paper**: \"Mapping the Predictability Boundary of Model Collapse: Calibration Canaries and Regime-Dependent Early Warning in Iterative Synthetic Data Training\"\n\n**Review mode**: full (2 reviewers, complete roundtable)\n\n---\n\n## R1: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThe paper proposes a Toda-Yamamoto (TY) panel temporal precedence framework to identify which metrics predict model collapse in iterative self-consuming training across contamination regimes. The experimental coverage spans 8 experiments (α-sweep, methodological ablation, cross-domain, scale spot-check, extended run, CRS intervention, data strategy ablation). However, the core statistical methodology is applied to extremely short time series (10–20 data points), raising fundamental concerns about inference validity.\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: **Systematic experimental design.** All 8 planned experiments are reported, covering a well-structured matrix (4 canary families × 2+ targets × 4 α values × 3–4 seeds).\n- S2: **Honest null-result reporting.** Two of four canary families (effective_rank, self_BLEU) fail across all conditions. These are reported in full with plausible failure mechanisms rather than omitted.\n- S3: **Methodological ablation is independently valuable.** Demonstrating that naive Pearson correlation inflates significance substantially on non-stationary training trajectories (Table 3) is a genuine cautionary contribution for future model collapse studies.\n- S4: **Phase diagram framing is intellectually honest.** The paper explicitly maps where canary signals work and where they fail, rather than overclaiming universal predictive power.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: **(Critical) Degrees-of-freedom issue with TY on 10-point series.** A bivariate VAR(k + d_max) with k=2 has ~14 parameters. With T=10, the effective sample size for estimation is 6–7 points — severely over-parameterized. The Wald test's χ² approximation requires T >> parameters, which is not satisfied. This undermines all p-values in Tables 1–2. The 20-gen extended run partially alleviates this but covers only α=1.0 with 2 seeds.\n- W2: **(Major) No multiple comparisons correction.** Table 2 reports TY p-values for 32 tests (8 canary-target pairs × 4 α). No Bonferroni, Holm, or FDR correction is applied. The phase boundary claim relies on marginal associations (p≈0.04 at α=0.25) that would not survive correction.\n- W3: **(Major) \"≥2/3 seeds significant\" threshold is ad hoc.** With only 3 seeds, 2/3 is the minimum possible majority. Given inflated Type I error from W1, the joint false-positive probability is non-trivial. No formal panel test or meta-analytic combination is used.\n- W4: **(Major) Effect size \"r\" undefined.** The paper reports r > 0.88–0.94 without defining what \"r\" measures. If this is peak cross-correlation at optimal lag, it conflates shared non-stationary trend with genuine lead-lag structure and is not a meaningful temporal precedence effect size.\n- W5: **(Moderate) CRS intervention baseline is confounded.** CRS triggers on ECE threshold and reduces α, but a simple reactive baseline (check current-generation ECE, reduce α when it rises) would trigger at the same time. The value of temporal precedence prediction over reactive monitoring is not demonstrated.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: Can you validate TY p-values against bootstrap-based p-values or Monte Carlo simulations under the null at T=10?\n- Q2: Can you provide raw per-seed metric trajectories for independent verification?\n- Q3: What is the formal definition of effect size \"r\"?\n- Q4: Why does CRS require lead-time prediction rather than simple reactive monitoring?\n\n### Soundness: 2/4 | Presentation: 3/4 | Overall: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## R2: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper extends Du et al.'s informal observation that ECE precedes accuracy degradation in SC training into a systematic multi-metric, multi-regime framework using Toda-Yamamoto temporal precedence testing. The contribution is methodologically well-conceived but primarily incremental in novelty — formalizing and quantifying a qualitatively known phenomenon rather than uncovering a fundamentally new one.\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: **Well-structured research question.** The three-part decomposition — \"which metrics, how far ahead, under what conditions\" — provides a clean organizational framework for a space that prior work left informal.\n- S2: **The naive-vs-TY false-positive finding is broadly valuable.** Demonstrating ~20% spurious significance from naive correlation on non-stationary training metrics is a useful methodological warning independent of the specific canary results.\n- S3: **Regime-dependent framing turns null results into evidence.** Presenting the canary→perplexity failure as a mapped boundary rather than a limitation is scientifically mature.\n- S4: **Accumulate-extends-lead-time is a novel minor finding.** Connecting data strategy to monitoring window width is new and practically relevant.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: **(Major) Incremental over Du et al.** The headline finding — ECE leads downstream degradation — was already observed by Du et al. The extensions (formal TY testing, token entropy as second canary, regime dependence, panel comparison) are valuable but quantitatively refine a known qualitative observation. The distance between \"informal observation\" and \"formal TY test on the same phenomenon\" is narrower than the paper's framing implies.\n- W2: **(Major) \"Phase diagram\" framing overstated.** The paper has 4 discrete α values on a single axis with a \"boundary\" interpolated between α=0.50 and α=0.75. This is an α-sweep with a threshold observation, not a phase diagram in the physics sense. The precision claim of \"α* ≈ 0.60\" is unjustified; the data supports only \"somewhere in [0.50, 0.75].\" \"Regime-dependent analysis\" would be a more honest descriptor.\n- W3: **(Major) Critical missing related work.** The tipping-point early-warning literature (Scheffer et al., \"Early-warning signals for critical transitions,\" Nature 2009; Science 2012) is the intellectual ancestor of this work and is uncited. That body of work established general conditions under which early-warning indicators succeed or fail — precisely this paper's central question applied to a different domain.\n- W4: **(Moderate) TY is off-the-shelf, not methodological innovation.** Listing the TY procedure as a \"methodological contribution\" overstates the case. TY is a 1995 econometric technique. The contribution is appropriate application and the empirical finding that naive methods fail 20% of the time, not method development.\n- W5: **(Moderate) Narrow validation.** Two Pythia models (same family/tokenizer/pretraining), two English text datasets, autoregressive LMs only. Claims about the mechanism being \"model-intrinsic\" rest on one cross-domain check at a single α value. At minimum, one architecturally distinct model is needed.\n- W6: **(Moderate) Additional missing references.** Seddik et al. (2024) on theoretical collapse in Gaussian mixtures; Gerstgrasser et al. (2024) on accumulation strategies; Ovadia et al. (NeurIPS 2019) on calibration under distribution shift; Tank et al. (IEEE TPAMI 2022) on neural Granger causality.\n- W7: **(Minor) CRS is trivially obvious.** Monitor ECE, reduce α when it crosses threshold — this is the most obvious intervention. Giving it a subsection and abstract prominence is disproportionate.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: How does the predictability boundary relate to critical slowing down (Scheffer et al.)?\n- Q2: What is the incremental actionable insight beyond Du et al.'s \"monitor ECE\" recommendation?\n- Q3: Can you provide theoretical or empirical argument for why α* generalizes beyond Pythia + WikiText?\n\n### Novelty: 2/4 | Significance: 3/4 | Overall: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Discussion\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\nR1 and R2 exchanged reviews. Key observations:\n\n- **Full agreement on overall assessment (3/6, Weak Reject)** — arrived at independently.\n- **Complementary concerns**: R1's statistical rigor issues (DOF, multiple comparisons, bootstrap validation) and R2's novelty/positioning concerns (incremental over Du et al., missing Scheffer et al., phase diagram overstatement) are orthogonal and mutually reinforcing.\n- **Shared positive assessment**: Both highlight honest null-result reporting and the methodological ablation as genuine value.\n- **Shared CRS concern**: R1 flags the confounded baseline; R2 flags the trivial intervention design. Both agree CRS prominence is disproportionate.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n\nAfter discussion, reviewers converge on the following:\n\n1. **The research question is important and well-posed.** Understanding which metrics predict collapse and under what conditions is a valuable question for the model collapse community.\n2. **The framework design is sound in conception** but the statistical execution has serious gaps (DOF at T=10, no multiple comparisons correction, undefined effect size).\n3. **Novelty is incremental** — the paper's primary contribution is formalizing Du et al.'s observation with appropriate (but off-the-shelf) time-series methodology.\n4. **The strongest contribution is the methodological ablation** (naive vs TY, 20% overturned), which provides an independently valuable cautionary result.\n5. **The paper has a clear path to improvement**: longer generation horizons (20+ as default), bootstrap-calibrated p-values, FDR correction, finer α grid near boundary, missing references (Scheffer et al.), and an architecturally distinct model would address the main concerns.\n6. **The phase diagram framing should be softened** to \"regime-dependent analysis\" — the data has 4 α values, insufficient for phase-diagram language.\n\n### Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Verdict**: The paper addresses a genuinely important question — mapping the predictability boundaries of model collapse — with a well-designed experimental framework. The honest reporting of negative results and the methodological ablation showing naive correlation's unreliability are real contributions. However, two interacting categories of concern prevent acceptance:\n\n**(A) Statistical rigor (R1):** The Toda-Yamamoto procedure on 10-point time series operates outside its validity regime. With ~14 VAR parameters and 6–7 effective observations, the χ² approximation underlying all reported p-values is unreliable. The absence of multiple comparisons correction is problematic given that the phase boundary claim hinges on marginal p-values (p≈0.04) at α=0.25. The effect size metric is undefined. These are not minor presentation issues — they undermine confidence in the quantitative claims.\n\n**(B) Incremental novelty (R2):** The headline finding (ECE leads collapse) was qualitatively established by Du et al. The paper's extensions — formal testing, second canary family, regime characterization — are valuable but the combined novelty falls below the threshold for a top venue. The missing connection to the tipping-point early-warning literature (Scheffer et al.) is a significant positioning gap. The \"phase diagram\" framing overpromises relative to 4 discrete α values.\n\n**Key strengths worth preserving in revision:**\n- Systematic canary comparison with honest negative results\n- Methodological ablation showing 20% spurious associations\n- Regime-dependent framing of predictability\n\n**Required for revision:**\n1. Bootstrap or permutation-based p-values to validate TY at T=10 (or increase T to 20+ as default)\n2. FDR correction across the full test battery\n3. Formal definition and justification of effect size metric\n4. Cite and position within Scheffer et al.'s tipping-point framework\n5. Finer α grid (6+ values) to justify \"phase diagram/boundary\" language, or soften to \"regime analysis\"\n6. At least one non-Pythia model architecture\n7. Reactive intervention baseline for CRS comparison\n8. Soften \"phase diagram\" to \"regime-dependent analysis\" unless finer grid is provided\n\n**Scores Summary:**\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consolidated |\n|-----------|----|----|-------------|\n| Soundness | 2/4 | — | 2/4 |\n| Novelty | — | 2/4 | 2/4 |\n| Significance | — | 3/4 | 2.5/4 |\n| Presentation | 3/4 | — | 3/4 |\n| Overall | 3/6 | 3/6 | **3/6 (Weak Reject)** |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n","project":"distrib_canary_collapse","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260514T184925","overall_score":"3/6 Weak Reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8914,"cache_read_input_tokens":53301,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8914},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b658381-150c-4d7a-982d-1cebf7ec2b62","uuid":"daeaca0c-1898-47ce-95d6-9b6ea5a6151c"}
